[hermes:err-live] #### Issue body
[hermes:err-live] Tracking issue: #51796
[hermes:err-live] > Master-breaking (no rolling-upgrade shim). Under review by @bigsheeper (this reverses the barrier-ts half of #50577).
[hermes:err-live] >
[hermes:err-live] > Rebased onto master and squashed. Now also carries #51879 — see "Removing the collection index meta" below.
[hermes:err-live] ## What this does
[hermes:err-live] QueryNode decided whether to apply a schema payload by consulting three different values: `schema.Version` from RootCoord, a `schemaBarrierTs` derived from the collection's `UpdateTimestamp`, and a node-local `segcoreSchemaVersion` counter fed to C++ segcore. They meant different things, seeded each other for rolling compatibility, and were compared in combination, so which one fenced what could not be read off the code.
[hermes:err-live] This collapses them to `schema.Version` alone — the Go-side freshness key and the value segcore gates on (#50577). `LoadMetaInfo.schema_barrier_ts` and `UpdateSchemaRequest.schema_barrier_ts` are left defined but dead for wire compatibility; the in-flight load fence moves to the version domain; `Schema::ParseFrom` now sources `schema_version_` from the proto, which also fixes a previously uninitialized member.
[hermes:err-live] **Reopen no longer advances the delegator's served schema** (#50989 / #51062). That advance was suppressing the stream `UpdateSchema` that follows it, and with it the derived state only that path rebuilds — the idfOracle and the function runners. A reopened segment decodes with whichever of the request and served schema is newer, and configures its output with that same schema, because QueryCoord can snapshot an older collection version while the node has already advanced the segment from the WAL.
[hermes:err-live] ## Property alters now bump schema.Version
[hermes:err-live] Making `schema.Version` the only ordering key exposed a second problem. `mmap.enabled`, `warmup.*` and `partitionkey.isolation` are materialized into the effective load schema by QueryCoord (`applyCollectionSettings` writes them into field `TypeParams`), but QueryNode then dropped that snapshot as a same-version no-op — so on an already-loaded collection they took effect only after a full release and load. Before this series they still worked, riding in on the update timestamp; removing that left them with no path at all.
[hermes:err-live] Two invariants now hold:
[hermes:err-live] - **Every schema-carrying broadcast gets its `+1` from `nextSchemaSnapshot`**, the single site that computes it, replacing ten sites across six files — twice with the mask append and the bump in different functions. A test walks the package AST and fails on any other assignment to `schema.Version` (a literal `0` is a create-time seed, not a bump).
[hermes:err-live] - **The decision to broadcast is derived from the snapshot's content**, not from a list of property keys, so a property added later is covered without anyone registering it. Only `collection.replica.number` and `collection.resource_groups` are excluded: QueryCoord consumes them through `AlterLoadConfig`, they never reach the effective schema, and elastic resize rewrites them often enough that bumping would fence in-flight segment loads for nothing. Everything unclassified bumps, so mis-classifying a key costs an extra broadcast rather than a silently dropped setting.
[hermes:err-live] `collection.ttl.seconds` is a query-time value carried in the request (`CollectionTtlTimestamps`), never decoded from the schema, so it does not reach the snapshot and does not bump.
[hermes:err-live] ## Removing the collection index meta (#51879)
[hermes:err-live] The collection-wide `CollectionIndexMeta` is gone from QueryNode entirely.
[hermes:err-live] It was a per-node mutable global with no ordering authority: every writer published a whole `ListIndexes` snapshot that QueryCoord had taken at task-pack time, so a late older one could roll back a newer one and make `CreateSearchPlanByExpr` reject an **entire shard** with `FieldNotLoaded` — for a field whose index the segments had already loaded. The gate never guaranteed what it appeared to either, since a field present in that snapshot may still be missing from any given segment (`add_field` before backfill reaches every segment), which…
